    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-47031-6",
    "abstract": "Understanding the effects of land-use and land-cover change (LULC) on carbon emissions is crucial for ensuring the sustainable management of dryland ecosystems. This study investigates spatial and temporal changes in carbon stock associated with LULC in one of the key biodiversity hotspots of the West African drylands. The study uses a Landsat imagery dataset spanning 2000, 2010, and 2022, processed in Google Earth Engine using a random forest algorithm. LULC-induced changes in carbon stock are estimated using ecosystem-specific emission factors for the designated study area. Additionally, integrating NDVI and climate stressors enables the projection of vegetation productivity and future carbon trajectories. The findings indicate a substantial decline in wooded savanna (~ 21%), tree savanna (7%), and shrub savanna (5%), alongside a ~ 35% increase in cropland. These transitions generated an estimated 300,000 MgC of carbon stock changes between 2000 and 2022. Climate projections under SSP1-2.6 and SSP5-8.5 scenarios indicate continued warming and reductions in annual rainfall of 5.414 mm and 9.359 mm, respectively, leading to additional carbon increases of ~ 1.3 MgC/ha and ~ 2 MgC/ha by 2070. These combined pressures accentuate land degradation and climate feedbacks. It emphasizes the need for integrated strategies to enhance climate resilience in West African dryland ecosystems in line with Sustainable Development Goal (SDG 15).",
    "field": "Carbon emissions from land-use change",
    "problem": "The contribution of land-use change to carbon stock dynamics and climate variability in West African drylands is insufficiently quantified, with limited spatially explicit information linking land-use transitions, vegetation dynamics, and future climate scenarios, hindering sustainable management and resilience efforts.",
    "method": "This study integrates remote sensing analysis, carbon accounting using ecosystem-specific emission factors, and climate projections to assess historical and future carbon stock changes driven by land-use dynamics in West African drylands.",
    "task": "The task is to assess the spatiotemporal dynamics of carbon emissions induced by land-use change and project future carbon trajectories under different climate scenarios to understand implications for climate resilience in West African drylands.",
    "model": [
        "CMIP6"
    ],
